                                        User Management

--> users help linux control who can access what and what they are allowed to do.
--> Types of users in Linux:

1. Root user:
--> the root user/super user is the administrator of Linux.
--> root user has almost complete control over the system.

2. Standard user:
--> A standard user is create for a human.
--> they  have liited permission compared to root account.
eg: seema, atul etc

3. System user:
--> system user is a user created by system rather than a regular users who used to run background processes and services

adduser/useradd <username>
--> used to create standard user
adduser seema

**Important files:

1. /etc/passwd : store all user information
2. /etc/shells : store all system supported shell list
3. /etc/shadow : store all user passswords

**User Information:
seema  :  x  :  1000  :  1000  :  seema patil,101,,  :  /home/seema  :  /bin/bash
  1       2      3         4             5                 6                7

  1 : User name
  2 : password
  3 : user ID
  4 : Group ID
  5 : User Information
  6 : User's home directory
  7 : Shell type 

  usermod -u <new_uid> <user_name>
  --> used to change user id of user
  usermod -u 7001 seema

  usermod -g <new_gid> <user_name>
  --> used to change group id of user
  usermod -g 1001 seema

  usermod -c "comment" <user_name>
  --> used to modify user information
  usermod -c "seema patil, 101, 0123456789, Mumbai, devops engg" seema

  usermod -d <new_directory> <user_name>
  --> used to change users working directory
  usermod -d /mnt seema

  usermod -s <new_shell> <user_name>
  --> used to change shell type of user
  usermod -s /bin/sh seema

  usermod -l <new_user_name> <old_user_name>
  --> to rename user name
  usermod -l seema1 seema

 passwd <user_name>
 --> to change user password
 passwd seema

 passwd
 --> if user want to change its own password

